    <h2 id="s1">The short answer</h2>
    <p>Heli-ski etiquette comes down to a few principles: <strong>follow the guide, ski your turn and stay on the group's line, be disciplined around the helicopter, be ready at transitions, and look out for the others</strong>. Do these and you are safe, efficient and a pleasure to ski with.</p>
    <p>It is not complicated, but it matters more than in resort skiing because the environment is serious and the group depends on each other. Heliski Travel briefs you on all of it before you go, and as the authorised agent, booking through us costs exactly the same as going direct. Here is what good etiquette looks like.</p>

    <h2 id="s2">Around the helicopter</h2>
    <p>The landing zone is where discipline matters most, and the rules are simple and strict. <strong>Always follow the guide's and pilot's directions exactly.</strong> Approach and leave the helicopter only when signalled, stay low, and keep well clear of the tail rotor, which is the single most important safety rule.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Hold onto <strong>loose items</strong>, hats, gloves, straps, so nothing blows into the rotors.</li>
      <li>Load and unload <strong>calmly and quickly</strong>, keeping your skis and poles as directed.</li>
      <li>Never move around the aircraft without the guide's signal.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>None of this is difficult, but it must be second nature. The guide will brief it thoroughly before your first flight, and following it precisely is the clearest sign of a good guest.</p>

    <h2 id="s3">Follow the guide</h2>
    <p>The guide is in charge, and for good reason: an <strong>IFMGA-certified professional</strong> is reading the snowpack, choosing terrain and managing the group's safety all day. Following their lead is not just courtesy, it is the foundation of a safe trip.</p>
    <p>That means skiing where and when they say, stopping where they indicate, and accepting their calls, including the decision not to ski a line. If you have a question or a preference, raise it at a stop rather than acting on your own. Trusting the guide is the mark of an experienced group member. More on their role in our guide to <a href="/heliskiing-guide/heliskiing-safety-guides">guides and safety</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s4">Ski your turn and your line</h2>
    <p>In the backcountry, when and where you ski matters. <strong>Ski your turn</strong>, going when the guide indicates, not before, and <strong>stay on the group's line</strong>, following the track the guide has set rather than cutting your own across the slope.</p>
    <p>There are real reasons for this: the guide chooses lines to avoid terrain traps, manage the snowpack and reach the pickup point. Skiing out of turn or off-line can trigger hazards, ski above other group members, or strand you away from the group. Predictable, disciplined skiing keeps everyone safe and the day flowing.</p>

    <h2 id="s5">Sharing the powder fairly</h2>
    <p>Untracked snow is the whole point, and part of good etiquette is <strong>sharing it fairly</strong>. Guides manage this well, spreading first tracks across the group over the day, but your part is to resist the urge to poach fresh snow outside your line or turn.</p>
    <p>Ski the excellent snow you are given, let others have theirs, and trust that over a day and a week it evens out. A group where everyone grabs the best snow for themselves is neither safe nor enjoyable; a group that shares generously has a far better time. It is one of the quiet pleasures of skiing with good people.</p>

    <h2 id="s6">Be ready at transitions</h2>
    <p>Heli-skiing has a rhythm of skiing and transitions, arriving at pickups, loading, unloading, regrouping, and a smooth day depends on everyone being <strong>ready and efficient</strong> at these moments. Fumbling for gear, not being packed, or holding up the group at the helicopter costs everyone skiing time.</p>
    <p>Keep your kit organised, know where your things are, and be prompt at each transition. Small efficiencies add up across a day to more runs and more vertical for the whole group. Being the person who is always ready is quietly appreciated by everyone.</p>

    <h2 id="s7">Honesty about ability</h2>
    <p>Good etiquette starts before the trip, with <strong>honesty about your skiing level</strong> when you book. Overstating your ability to reach better terrain is not just a personal risk, it affects the whole group, forcing compromises or slowing the day when someone is out of their depth.</p>
    <p>Describe your skiing accurately and you will be matched with a group of similar pace and put on terrain that suits you, which makes the day better for everyone. It is both the safest and the most considerate thing you can do. Our <a href="/field-notes/heliskiing-mistakes-to-avoid">mistakes-to-avoid guide</a> covers why this matters so much.</p>

    <h2 id="s8">Attitude on weather days</h2>
    <p>Weather is part of every heli trip, and how you handle a down day says a lot. Groups sometimes lose a day to conditions, and the <strong>guaranteed-vertical model</strong> means your skiing is not lost, it carries over. A patient, positive attitude keeps the whole group's morale up.</p>
    <p>Grumbling about the weather helps no one and dampens the trip for everyone. The best group members take down days in stride, enjoy the lodge or the town, and are ready to go the moment the mountains open. That attitude is contagious in the best way. Read more on <a href="/field-notes/weather-and-down-days-heliskiing">weather and down days</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s9">Looking out for the group</h2>
    <p>Above all, good etiquette is about <strong>looking out for each other</strong>. Keep an eye on the group members around you, flag anything that seems off to the guide, help at transitions, and be encouraging to those finding a run challenging. Heli-skiing in a small group builds a real camaraderie when everyone contributes to it.</p>
    <p>The best trips are the ones where the group gels, and that comes from everyone putting the group first. Be that person, and you will not only have a better time yourself, you will be the one others hope to ski with again.</p>

    <h2 id="s10">How to prepare</h2>
    <p>Preparing to be a great group member is easy: arrive fit and honest about your ability, read the briefings, and go in with a team mindset. If it is your first trip, that is exactly what the pre-trip briefing and your guide are there to help with. Tell Heliski Travel your level and any questions, and we will make sure you arrive prepared. As the <strong>authorised booking agent for Viking Heliskiing</strong>, booking through us costs exactly the same as going direct, and we reply within 12 hours.</p>
    <p>When you are ready, <a href="/packages">browse the packages</a>, or <a href="/contact">request a tailored quote</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s11">The verdict</h2>
    <p>Heli-ski etiquette is simply the behaviour that keeps a small group safe, efficient and happy in a serious environment: follow the guide, ski your turn, be disciplined around the helicopter, share the snow, be ready, be honest and stay positive. It is mostly common sense, and it makes a real difference to the day.</p>
    <p>Be a great group member and the whole trip is better, for you and everyone else. When you are ready, <a href="/packages">browse the packages</a> or <a href="/contact">request a quote</a>. We reply within 12 hours.</p>

    <h2 id="s12">Thanking the guides and pilots</h2>
    <p>The guides and pilots work hard, and much of what they do to keep the day safe and excellent goes unseen, the snow assessment, the terrain calls, the flying. A simple, genuine <strong>thank you</strong> at the end of a day or trip goes a long way, and is always appreciated.</p>
    <p>Where tipping is customary, it is a normal way to show appreciation for exceptional service, though it is never obligatory and is always at your discretion. If you are unsure of the local norm, just ask us and we will guide you. Beyond money, being a positive, engaged group member and acknowledging the team's skill is its own form of thanks, and it is the kind guides remember most warmly.</p>

    <h2 id="s13">Cameras and phones on the mountain</h2>
    <p>Everyone wants to capture a heli trip, and you should, the footage is spectacular. But there is an etiquette to it. <strong>Skiing comes first, filming second</strong>: never stop in an unsafe place for a photo, never hold up the group or delay a transition for a shot, and never let a camera distract you on serious terrain.</p>
    <p>Keep phones and cameras <strong>secured</strong> so nothing drops on a descent or blows into the rotors at the helicopter. Follow the guide's lead on when and where it is safe to film, and be considerate about photographing other guests. Done sensibly, you will come home with extraordinary images and a group that enjoyed the day, rather than one that spent it waiting for you. Our <a href="/field-notes/heliski-photography-tips">heli photography tips</a> cover how to get the shots safely.</p>

